# Summary of changes for run 10df4dc2-36a3-4bdc-b224-459bb5e161bf
Continued and extended the pole-order obstruction research thread with three further research cycles, all machine-checked.

**New Lean files (all build cleanly, zero `sorry`, axioms limited to `propext`, `Classical.choice`, `Quot.sound`)**

- `Catalog/Shared/PoleOrderObstructionDimension.lean` — the *linear* face of the pole-order invariant:
  - `poleSpace m`, the ℂ-subspace of Laurent series with at most a pole of order `m`, with the valuation characterization `x ∈ poleSpace m ↔ -m ≤ orderTop x`;
  - `poleSpace_mul_mem` — the filtration is multiplicative (pole orders add);
  - `polePartEquiv` / `finrank_polePartSpace` — the space of principal parts of pole order ≤ `m` (image of `poleSpace m` in `ℂ⸨q⸩ / poleSpace 0`) is isomorphic to `Fin m → ℂ` and so has dimension exactly `m`;
  - `finrank_gradedPiece` — every graded piece `poleSpace (m+1) / poleSpace m` is one-dimensional (a formal-Laurent analogue of the Riemann–Roch jump `ℓ(D) − ℓ(D−P) ≤ 1`);
  - `prod_traceLaurent_194_notMem_poleSpace_193` and `principalPart_prod_traceLaurent_194_top` — the Monster-sized product lies in `poleSpace 194` but not `poleSpace 193`, with deepest principal-part coordinate `1`.

- `Catalog/Shared/PoleOrderObstructionLattice.lean` — the value-group interpolation, closing two conjectures from the previous cycle:
  - `exists_root_support_subset_iff` — for *any* set of rational exponents containing ℤ and closed under addition, the Monster-sized product has an `n`-th root supported in that set iff the single exponent `−194/n` belongs to it;
  - `exists_lattice_root_iff` — specialized to the lattice `(1/N)ℤ`: an `n`-th root exists iff `n ∣ 194·N` (`N = 1` recovers the ℤ-graded answer `n ∣ 194`);
  - `lattice_root_iff_replicate_root` — value-group refinement and replication depth are the *same* invariant;
  - sample instances: `exists_lattice_root_one_iff`, `exists_lattice_cube_root_iff` (a cube root appears exactly when `3 ∣ N`).

**Documents updated at the project root**: `ComputationalEvidence.md` (new sections on principal-part dimensions and the lattice interpolation table, each item cross-referenced to the Lean theorem that proves it), `FUTURE_DIRECTIONS.md` (updated analysis, six prose conjectures with "key insight"/"Why now?" sentences and a machine-readable `future_directions.json` block whose titles are noun phrases; conjectures closed by the new work were replaced by sharper successors, including the associated-graded-algebra and Kummer-theory-over-general-value-groups conjectures), and `SELF_EVALUATION.json`.

No forbidden Phase A packaging files were created, no axioms or `@[implemented_by]` were introduced, and the earlier user-provided files were left untouched. All work is committed and pushed.

concept.concept_description:
The current theorem is an exact Hahn-series statement. To connect it to analysis,
formalize an interpretation into germs of real functions and prove:

1. the leading nonzero monomial controls eventual sign;
2. smaller ranks are asymptotically negligible relative to larger ranks;
3. the interpretation is injective on a normalized, summable fragment;
4. formal agreement to all ranks implies equality of eventual germs.

This will distinguish the valid formal principle from the generally false claim
that arbitrary smooth functions with identical classical asymptotic power series
must coincide (flat functions provide counterexamples).

